= Operating system kernel =
<onlyinclude>An '''operating system kernel''' is the privileged core of an [[Operating system]] that mediates access to processor execution, memory and devices, and implements critical protection and resource-management mechanisms. The kernel is not the entire operating system: user-space services, applications and utilities generally operate outside it.</onlyinclude>

== Responsibilities ==
* '''Execution and scheduling:''' represent runnable tasks, coordinate context switches, and arbitrate processor time.
* '''Memory:''' manage physical pages, virtual-address mappings and faults, with hardware-assisted isolation where supported.
* '''System-call interface:''' validate requests from less-privileged programs and mediate privileged operations.
* '''Interrupts and devices:''' handle asynchronous hardware events and coordinate device access, often through kernel drivers.
* '''Protection:''' enforce privilege and address-space boundaries subject to correct implementation and hardware behavior.

== Design choices ==
Monolithic kernels implement many services in privileged address space; microkernels seek to minimize privileged mechanisms and place more services outside the kernel. Neither label alone establishes security, correctness or performance. Hybrid designs blur this classification.

== Boundaries and limitations ==
Kernel protection does not establish end-to-end security: firmware, hardware, privileged drivers, configuration and user-space components remain relevant. Different kernels distribute services differently. Linux-specific interfaces should not be mistaken for universal kernel requirements.

== Related knowledge ==
[[Operating system]] describes the broader software system; [[Computer architecture]] provides hardware execution and privilege mechanisms; [[Computer security]] addresses protection beyond the kernel boundary.

